WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.530
Dependency management requires knowing what software the application uses and where it is deployed.

2
00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:12.796
Without inventory, teams cannot reliably assess exposure when a new vulnerability is announced.

3
00:00:12.796 --> 00:00:18.403
Unsupported software is difficult to defend because fixes may no longer be available.

4
00:00:18.403 --> 00:00:26.911
Legacy frameworks, old runtimes, abandoned packages, and unmaintained plugins can become permanent risk unless they are replaced.

5
00:00:26.911 --> 00:00:33.507
Third-party risk includes both known vulnerabilities and the possibility of supply chain compromise.

6
00:00:33.507 --> 00:00:41.290
Packages, build tools, images, and vendor components should be evaluated as part of the application security boundary.

7
00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:45.710
Version updates must be paired with testing and release discipline.

8
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:54.153
The goal is to remediate exploitable risk quickly without creating instability that causes teams to delay patching indefinitely.

9
00:00:54.153 --> 00:00:57.714
Maintain software inventory and dependency visibility.

10
00:00:57.714 --> 00:01:01.210
Use vulnerability monitoring and dependency scanning.

11
00:01:01.210 --> 00:01:05.629
Patch, upgrade, or isolate vulnerable components according to risk.

12
00:01:05.629 --> 00:01:15.589
Selected NIST mappings for this topic include SI-2 - Flaw Remediation; CM-8 - System Component Inventory; RA-5 - Vulnerability Monitoring and Scanning.

